workedExamples.question_text:
The velocity–time graph shows a particle moving in a straight line for $0\le t\le8$ seconds. Read from the graph when it is at rest and when it moves in the negative direction.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-0.5,xmax=8.6,ymin=-4.8,ymax=13.5,xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8},ytick={-4,4,8,12},xlabel={$t$ (s)},ylabel={$v$ (m/s)},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},axis line style={black},tick style={black},clip=false]
\addplot[black,thick,samples=101,domain=0:8] {(x-2)*(x-6)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The graph meets the time axis at $t=2$ s and $t=6$ s, so the particle is at rest at those times. It lies below the time axis for $2<t<6$, so the particle moves in the negative direction on that interval.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{motionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-0.5,xmax=8.6,ymin=-4.8,ymax=13.5,xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8},ytick={-4,4,8,12},xlabel={$t$ (s)},ylabel={$v$ (m/s)},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},axis line style={black},tick style={black},clip=false]
\addplot[black,thick,samples=101,domain=0:8] {(x-2)*(x-6)};
\addplot[motionblue,very thick,samples=51,domain=2:6] {(x-2)*(x-6)};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,black] coordinates {(2,0) (6,0)};
\node[anchor=south west,xshift=2mm,yshift=3mm,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:2,0) {$(2,0)$};
\node[anchor=south east,xshift=-2mm,yshift=3mm,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:6,0) {$(6,0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The displacement–time graph shows a particle moving in a straight line for $0\le t\le7$ seconds. Read when the particle is at rest and when it is at the origin. Explain the difference.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-0.5,xmax=7.6,ymin=-8.5,ymax=10.5,xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7},ytick={-6,-3,3,6,9},xlabel={$t$ (s)},ylabel={$x$ (m)},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},axis line style={black},tick style={black},clip=false]
\addplot[black,thick,samples=101,domain=0:7] {6*x-x*x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The graph has a horizontal tangent at its turning point, $t=3$ s. Its gradient, and hence the velocity, is zero there, so the particle is at rest. Its displacement then is $9$ m.
The graph meets the time axis at $t=0$ and $t=6$ s, so the particle is at the origin at those times. Being at rest concerns the graph's gradient; being at the origin concerns its displacement value.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{motionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{axis}[width=7cm,height=5.5cm,axis lines=middle,xmin=-0.5,xmax=7.6,ymin=-8.5,ymax=10.5,xtick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7},ytick={-6,-3,3,6,9},xlabel={$t$ (s)},xlabel style={at={(axis description cs:1,0.447368)},anchor=north east,yshift=-8mm},ylabel={$x$ (m)},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},axis line style={black},tick style={black},clip=false]
\addplot[black,thick,samples=101,domain=0:7] {6*x-x*x};
\addplot[motionblue,thick,domain=2:4] {9};
\addplot[only marks,mark=*,black] coordinates {(0,0) (3,9) (6,0)};
\node[anchor=south,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:3,9) {$(3,9)$};
\node[anchor=south west,xshift=2mm,yshift=3mm,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\node[anchor=south east,xshift=-2mm,yshift=3mm,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] at (axis cs:6,0) {$(6,0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
